=== Module 25 - The LL(1) Parsing Table ===

M[A][t] = the production to apply when expanding A with lookahead t.
build: for each A -> alpha, put it in M[A][t] for every t in PREDICT(A -> alpha).
a cell that gets two productions is a CONFLICT  <=>  grammar is not LL(1).

########## fixtures/expr.grammar

productions:
  (0) E -> T Ep
  (1) Ep -> + T Ep
  (2) Ep -> epsilon
  (3) T -> F Tp
  (4) Tp -> * F Tp
  (5) Tp -> epsilon
  (6) F -> ( E )
  (7) F -> id
  (8) F -> num

PREDICT sets:
  (0) E -> T Ep            { (, id, num }
  (1) Ep -> + T Ep         { + }
  (2) Ep -> epsilon        { ), $ }
  (3) T -> F Tp            { (, id, num }
  (4) Tp -> * F Tp         { * }
  (5) Tp -> epsilon        { ), +, $ }
  (6) F -> ( E )           { ( }
  (7) F -> id              { id }
  (8) F -> num             { num }

table (cells = production index; . = blank, ! = conflict):
        +   *   (   )   id  num $   
  E     .   .   0   .   0   0   .   
  Ep    1   .   .   2   .   .   2   
  T     .   .   3   .   3   3   .   
  Tp    5   4   .   5   .   .   5   
  F     .   .   6   .   7   8   .   

VERDICT: LL(1) = YES   every cell has at most one production

########## fixtures/stmts.grammar

productions:
  (0) P -> L
  (1) L -> S L
  (2) L -> epsilon
  (3) S -> id assign E semi
  (4) E -> id
  (5) E -> num

PREDICT sets:
  (0) P -> L               { id, $ }
  (1) L -> S L             { id }
  (2) L -> epsilon         { $ }
  (3) S -> id assign E semi { id }
  (4) E -> id              { id }
  (5) E -> num             { num }

table (cells = production index; . = blank, ! = conflict):
        id     assign semi   num    $      
  P     0      .      .      .      0      
  L     1      .      .      .      2      
  S     3      .      .      .      .      
  E     4      .      .      5      .      

VERDICT: LL(1) = YES   every cell has at most one production

########## fixtures/danglingelse.grammar

productions:
  (0) S -> other
  (1) S -> if b then S Sp
  (2) Sp -> else S
  (3) Sp -> epsilon

PREDICT sets:
  (0) S -> other           { other }
  (1) S -> if b then S Sp  { if }
  (2) Sp -> else S         { else }
  (3) Sp -> epsilon        { else, $ }

table (cells = production index; . = blank, ! = conflict):
        other if    b     then  else  $     
  S     0     1     .     .     .     .     
  Sp    .     .     .     .     !     3     

VERDICT: LL(1) = NO    1 conflicted cell(s):
  M[Sp][else] wants:
      (2) Sp -> else S
      (3) Sp -> epsilon

using the expr table to parse  id + id * id :
   0  expand (0) E -> T Ep
   1  expand (3) T -> F Tp
   2  expand (7) F -> id
   3  match id
   4  expand (5) Tp -> epsilon
   5  expand (1) Ep -> + T Ep
   6  match +
   7  expand (3) T -> F Tp
   8  expand (7) F -> id
   9  match id
  10  expand (4) Tp -> * F Tp
  11  match *
  12  expand (7) F -> id
  13  match id
  14  expand (5) Tp -> epsilon
  15  expand (2) Ep -> epsilon
  16  stack top $     look $     ACCEPT
